## A B S T R A C T
In this study, cellulose acetate (CA)-based nanofiltration membranes, modified with zeolitic imidazole framework-8 (ZIF-8) particles, were prepared with various ZIF-8 contents (0, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1 and $2\ \mathrm{wt\%}\dot{}$ ), to obtain membranes with improved flux and filtration performance by combining advantages of CA polymer and ZIF-8 metal-organic frameworks. Removal efficiency studies were carried out with bovine serum albumin and two different dyes, along with antifouling performance evaluation. Results of experiments disclosed that as the ZIF-8 ratio increased, the contact angle values decreased. With ZIF-8 addition, the pure water flux of the membranes increased. Besides, the flux recovery ratio value was approximately $85\%$ for the bare CA membrane, while it increased to above $90\%$ by blending ZIF-8. Also, in all ZIF-8 doped membranes, a fouling decrease was observed. Importantly, it was observed that the dye removal efficiency increased with the addition of ZIF-8 particles from 95.2 to $97.7~\%$ for Reactive Black 5 dye.
## 1. Introduction
In the last hundred years, the quality and quantity of water resources in the world have dropped away due to the dramatic increase in popu­ lation and parallel with industrialization (Ag˘tas¸ et al., 2021). Recently, due to rapid industrialization, uncontrollable wastewater discharge and stringent environmental standards, researchers' attention to advanced wastewater treatment technologies has been increased (Goh & Ismail, 2018).
The primary purpose of wastewater treatment is to reduce the con­ centrations of certain pollutants in wastewater, making the discharge of wastewater safer to the environment or making wastewater more suit­ able for reuse. Biological and physicochemical processes can be used for the treatment of wastewater. The choice of treatment method generally depends on the type of wastewater, the operating cost and whether the wastewater must be reused or not (Sakr et al., 2021). For example, when considering the treatment of textile industry wastewater, traditional treatment methods are not effective enough with the tightening of legal discharge limits. For this reason, membrane processes come to the fore with their efficient treatment and obtaining high-quality purified water (Keskin et al., 2021).
In membrane selection, it is often necessary to obtain a tradeoff in terms of flux, stability, selectivity, and high production cost. Membrane fouling is also one of the major problems limiting the long-time opera­ tion of membrane processes (Ying et al., 2017). As a result of the membrane fouling, there is a decrease in the membrane flux. Therefore, higher pressure and, accordingly, more energy are needed to keep the membrane flux constant. Since the high-cost cleaning process is required to mitigate the fouling, both the operating cost will increase and the process life-time will decrease (Díez & Rosal, 2020).
Since most pollutants that cause fouling are hydrophobic, it is thought that increasing the hydrophilicity of the membrane will improve the resistance of the membrane against fouling. Various methods are used to increase the hydrophilicity of polymeric mem­ branes. The most commonly used methods include chemical modifica­ tion, mixing hydrophilic polymers, plasma polymerization, and nanoparticle addition to the membrane casting solution (Pekgenc et al., 2022; Vatanpour et al., 2021). In recent years, the addition of nano­ particles to membranes has attracted researchers' attention due to the ease of application and suitable performance of the resultant nano­ composite membranes (Safarpour et al., 2016). Nanoparticles, such as graphene oxide, alumina, carbon nanotube, silica, and metal oxides, have been frequently used in literature (Rabiee et al., 2015; Safikhani et al., 2021).
Cellulose acetate (CA) polymer is likely to be an ideal biopolymer for membrane fabrication due to its low cost, biodegradability, easy manufacture, and hydrophilic structure advantages (Mu et al., 2017; Rajeswari et al., 2019; S. Yang et al., 2019). The most significant dis­ advantages of CA membranes are their high fouling tendency and low flux. Besides adsorption, other possible fouling mechanisms of mem­ branes include concentration polarization, pore blocking, and cake layer formation on the membrane surface (Bose et al., 2021). Another disad­ vantage of the CA-based ultrafiltration (UF) membranes is narrow operating ranges in terms of temperature and pH (Vatanpour et al., 2021).
To offset the disadvantages of these membranes produced from environmentally friendly polymers, research on membrane modification has been increased considerably in recent years. Different methods, including chemical grafting, surface modification, gamma irradiation, and plasma methods, are used to modify CA-UF membranes (Bose et al., 2021; S. Yang et al., 2021; X. T. Yuan et al., 2020). In addition to these methods, blending additives into membrane matrix is a promising modification alternative because it is straightforward (S. Yang et al., 2019). Easier membrane production also makes the scale up of the fabrication process to the industrial-scale more possible. For example, the effects of adding pore formers, nanoparticles, and zwitterionic molecules on membrane performance are indeed investigated as part of additive blending (Mu et al., 2017). Among these, the membrane hy­ drophilicity has been successfully improved by the addition of hydro­ philic nanoparticles. In addition, since many substances that cause membrane fouling are hydrophobic, the fouling could be mitigated by enhancing the membrane hydrophilicity (Vatanpour et al., 2021). In a report using titanium silicon oxide nanoparticles $\mathrm{(TiSiO_{4})}$ to prepare composite CA-UF membranes, enhancements were observed in hydro­ philicity, permeability, and fouling characteristics. In addition, the membrane hydraulic resistance was reduced by the addition of nano particles. In the membrane containing $20~\%$ nanoparticles, the pure water flux increased from 72 to $134~\mathrm{L~m}^{-2}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , while the removal of RR120 dye was $68\%$ . As a result of the addition of $\mathrm{TiSiO_{4}}$ doping, a UF membrane with high dye removal and resistance to high temperature was obtained for textile wastewater treatment (Dasgupta et al., 2014). In another study, organically modified montmorillonite (OMMt)-added CA-UF membranes were investigated to remove humic acids from drinking water. A 1.7-fold increase in pure water flux was observed by increasing the OMMt concentration from 0 to $5\%$ . Concurrently, with the flux improvement, hydrophilicity and porosity increased while mechanical and thermal stability decreased. The membrane containing $5\%$ OMMt achieved $95~\%$ humic acid removal (Sabeti Dehkordi et al., 2015). In addition to these studies, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ doped CA-UF membranes for bovine serum albumin (BSA) removal (Gebru & Das, 2017), and L-DOPA functionalized halloysite nanotube doped for egg Albumin removal (Mu et al., 2017). In addition to these nanosized materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOF) have been proposed due to their unparalleled physicochemical characteristics. Yang et al., for instance, fabricated graphene oxide (GO) and metal-organic framework-based GO doped CAUF for high flux and antifouling performance (S. Yang et al., 2019). Zeolitic Imidazole Framework-8 (ZIF-8) is another significant MOF used as an additive or modifier to enhance the UF membrane performance. In a previous study, the surface of Poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF)-based asymmetric UF membrane was modified with ZIF-8 to enhance filtration performance. Five hours of synthesis time of ZIF-8 resulted in the best flux enhancement performance, which reached 134 from $65.8\mathrm{~L~m~}^{-2}$ $\ensuremath{\mathbf{h}}^{-1}$ , and dye rejections were increased due to the negatively charged surface of the modified membrane (Karimi, Vatanpour, et al., 2019b). Not only for UF membrane, but also ZIF-8 is also a good MOF alternative for modifying polymeric NF membranes. When ZIF-8 was doped in the NF matrix with a $25\ \%$ ratio, it increased the Rose Bengal rejection as high as $99.5\%$ , corresponding to a $15\%$ increment compared to bare NF (Karimi, Khataee, Safarpour, et al., 2020a). In another study, ZIF-8 decorated with GO was modified with ammonium hydroxide and doped in the Poly(ether-sulfone) (PES) polymer matrix of the UF membrane. While $64\ \%$ pure water flux increment was observed, BSA rejection reached above $95\%$ . In addition to flux improvement, the flux recovery ratio $(F R R)$ of the ZIF-8 modified membrane was $84.4~\%$ , higher than that obtained for the pristine membrane $(57.1\ \%)$ (Ahmad et al., 2020). Based on these evidences, this study aims to obtain a UF membranes with improved flux and filtration performance by joining the strengths of CA polymer and ZIF-8 MOF. To the best of our knowl­ edge, there has been no previous study on the fabrication of ZIF-8 doped CA UF membranes in the literature. Herein, the morphological proper­ ties of the membranes, filtration performances by pure water flow, BSA and dye removal, and antifouling properties were investigated.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Cellulose acetate (CA, $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{n}}=50,000$ Da, $39.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ acetyl) was pur­ chased from Sigma Aldrich. $^{N,N}$ -dimethyl formamide (DMF) was ob­ tained from Ak-Kim Chemicals (Turkey). BSA and 2-methyl imidazole $(99~\%$ purity) were bought from Sigma-Aldrich. Polyethylene glycol (PEG) (Mw: $1000\textrm{\textit{g}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{mol}^{-1}})$ and zinc nitrate hexahydrate (Zn $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ was purchased from Merck (Germany). Reactive Black 5 $(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{w}=991.8~\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ and Reactive Red 120 $(\mathrm{Mw}=1136.3\ \mathrm{g\mol^{-1}})$ were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Pure water was used in the prep­ aration of dye and BSA solutions.
## 2.2. ZIF-8 characterization
SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy, TESCAN Czech Republic), ATRFTIR (Attenuated total reflectance-Fourier transform infrared, ABB Bomem-MB-104, Switzerland), and PXRD (Powder X-ray diffraction, X'PERT MRD, PANalytic, Holland), analyses were employed to charac­ terize the synthesized ZIF-8 structure.
## 2.3. Membrane characterization
Water contact angle tests were carried out to measure the hydro­ philicity of the produced membranes. For this, KSV Attension Theta measuring device was used. In order to ensure the accuracy, this mea­ surement was applied to each membrane more than once. Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FT-IR) (Spectrum 100, PerkinElmer, USA) device was used to determine the functional groups on the surface of the produced membranes. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Philips-XL30 SFEG) was used to examine the morphological structure of the resultant membranes. Membranes were coated with gold‑palladium for this process. The porosity measurement of the membranes was done by filling the membrane pores with distilled water. Eq. (1) was used for the calculation of the measurement, as follows;
$$
\varepsilon=\frac{W_{1}-W_{2}}{A\times L\times d_{w}}\times100\
$$
ε $(\%)$ : membrane porosity
$W_{1},W_{2}$ (g): weight of the wet and dry membranes A $(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ : surface area of the membrane $L$ (cm): thickness of the membrane $d_{w}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cm}}^{-3})$ : density of water
After filtration tests, the effluent was collected for color and BSA removal measurement by using UV spectroscopy (Hach Lange). The UV wavelength was chosen as $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ for Reactive Black, $520~\mathrm{nm}$ for Reactive Red, and 260 and $280\mathrm{nm}$ for BSA for measurement (Haghighat & Vatanpour, 2020). The mean pore size of the bare and composite membranes was calculated by using the Guerout-Elford-Ferry equation (Eq. (2)) (Alkhouzaam $\&$ Qiblawey, 2021).
$$
r_{m}=\sqrt{\frac{(2.9-1.75\varepsilon)\times8\eta L Q}{\varepsilon\times A\times\Delta P}}
$$
where ${\bf r}_{\mathrm{m}}$ (nm) represents the mean pore size, $\boldsymbol{\upeta}$ is the water viscosity $(9.3\times10^{-4}\mathrm{Pa}s_{\mathrm{.}}$ , at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , $\Delta\mathbf{P}$ is the operational pressure $\mathrm{(Pa)}$ , and $\mathsf{Q}$ is the permeate flow rate $(\mathbf{m}^{3}\thinspace s^{-1})$ .
## 2.4. Synthesis of ZIF-8 crystals
To synthesize the ZIF-8 particles, two aqueous solutions containing $2.93\ g\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $12.11\mathrm{~g~}$ distilled water, and $6.48\ g\ 2$ -methyli­ midazole $\operatorname{(Hmim)}$ in $48.45~\mathrm{g}$ distilled water were prepared. Both solu­ tions were vigorously stirred at room temperature. Then, the zinc nitrate solution was added to the Hmim solution and stirred magnetically for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The precipitated ZIF-8 particles were filtrated and washed with distilled water and ethanol several times before drying at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ (Nordin et al., 2014).
## 2.5. Membrane fabrication protocol
CA-based NF membranes were fabricated by the phase inversion method. The casting solution of the bare membrane consisted of $15\mathrm{wt\%}$ CA, $3\mathrm{wt\%}$ Polyethylene glycol 1000 (PEG1000) as pore former (related to polymer), and the rest Dimethylformamide (DMF), heated to $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , and stirred continuously. To prepare the MOF doped polymer so­ lution, a certain amount of ZIF-8 was first dispersed into DMF for 7 min by the ultrasonic probe, then PEG and half of the polymer were added and mixed vigorously for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ . The polymer solution was stirred continuously after adding the rest of the CA for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After complete dissolving, polymer solutions were kept at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ for degassing. Then, polymer solutions, which were cooled to room temperature, were poured on a clean and dry glass plate with a thickness of $150\mathrm{-}170~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and coagulated in a pure water bath at ambient temperature. While casting, the membrane was immersed in the coagulation bath without waiting as soon as the casting was done. At least four flat sheets were fabricated from each membrane. After $5\mathrm{min}$ of coagulation, the mem­ branes were separated from the glass plate and stored in clean distilled water. The prepared membrane formulations are listed in Table S1.
## 2.6. Membrane filtration performance
All performance tests of the membranes were performed with a quadruple dead-end filtration system. As shown in Fig. S1, the vessel filled with distilled water (no 1) was pressurized with nitrogen gas, and the pressurized flow was fed to four equal filtration cells. The active membrane area of the filtration cells was $12.6~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . Before the pure water flux measurements, the membranes were compacted at 5 bar for $20\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the pressure was reduced to 4 bar, and balance data were taken every $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Pure water flux was calculated using Eq. (3):
$$
J=\frac{V}{A\times\Delta t}
$$
where $J$ is the pure water flux (PWP) $(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ ; $V$ is the filtrate volume (L); $A$ is the active membrane area $(\mathbf{m}^{2})$ , and $\Delta t$ is the permeation time $\mathrm{(h)}$ between two successive time intervals. After 1 h of PWF experiment, $500~\mathrm{mg~L}^{-1}$ BSA solution was filtered through the membranes for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Then, samples were taken from both the permeate stream and inside the cell. BSA concentrations and removal efficiencies were calculated by Eqs. (4) and (5), respectively. Here, $E$ denotes the removal efficiency, and $C_{i n}$ and $C_{e f f}$ represent the influent and permeate concentrations, respectively (Haghighat $\&$ Vatanpour, 2020). A260 and A280 are absorbance values measured at 260 and $280\mathrm{nm}$ wavelengths.
$$
\mathrm{~E~}(\%)=\frac{C_{i n}-C_{e f f}}{C_{i n}}\times100\
$$
$$
B S A\left(m g L^{-1}\right)=A_{280}\times1000\times\left[-0.6373\times\left({\frac{A_{280}}{A_{260}}}\right)^{2}+2.1475\times\left({\frac{A_{280}}{A_{260}}}\right)-0,723\right]
$$
In addition to BSA, filtration performances were evaluated for two different azo dye removals, which are commonly used in textile industry (Ali et al., 2021; Al-Tohamy et al., 2020). Reactive Black 5 (RB5) dye solution $(\mathrm{M}\mathrm{w}=991.8~\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , $100\ \mathrm{mg\L^{-1}};$ was filtered for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ after washing the BSA filtered membrane. Afterward, the membrane surface was gently washed with distilled water and rinsed in distilled water for $20\mathrm{min}$ . Then, Reactive Red 120 (RR120) dye solution $(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{w}=1136.3\:\mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , $100\mathrm{mgL}^{-1})$ was filtered through the washed membrane for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . The fluxes were calculated by taking the balance data every $20\mathrm{min}$ for both dye filtrations. After filtration, ultraviolet (UV) spectroscopy ab­ sorbances of samples taken from both the filtrate and the cell inside were measured. Wavelength measurements were performed for RB5 and RR120 at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $520\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Removal yields were calculated according to Eq. (4). The inside concentration of the filtration cell after filtration was considered the influent concentration.
## 2.7. Antifouling
The following procedure was applied to examine the antifouling properties of pure and ZIF-8-doped CA membranes: After pure water filtration, the BSA-filtered membrane was gently washed with distilled water rinsed in pure water for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, pure water filtration was performed for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and the PWP after BSA was calculated as well. Flux recovery ratio $(F R R\%)$ , reversible fouling $\left({{R_{r}}\%}\right)$ , intrinsic resistance $(R_{m})$ , cake resistance $(R_{c})$ , pore resistance $(R_{p})$ and irreversible fouling $(R_{i r}\%)$ were calculated by Eqs. (6)–(11), respectively.
$$
F R R\%=\frac{J_{W1}}{J_{w}}\times100\
$$
$$
R_{m}=\frac{\varDelta P}{\mu J_{w}}
$$
$$
R_{p}=\frac{\varDelta\mathrm{P}}{\mu J_{w1}}-R_{m}
$$
$$
R_{c}=\frac{\varDelta P}{\mu J_{B S A}}-R_{m}-R_{f}
$$
$$
R_{r}\%=\left(\frac{J_{w1}-J_{B S A}}{J_{w}}\right)\times100\
$$
$$
R_{i r}\%=\left(\frac{J_{w}-J_{w1}}{J_{w}}\right)\times100\
$$
where $J_{w},\ J_{w l;}$ , and $J_{B S A}$ are PWP, PWP after BSA, and BSA flux, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of materials
Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) results (Fig. 1b) also indicate obvious bands certifying the formation of ZIF-8 structures in tune with similar study results (Denny & Cohen, 2015). Significant bands appeared at 1567, 2925, 3135, and $3181~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ , aliphatic $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ , aromatic C–H and stretching vibration of N–H of the 2-methyl imidazole in ZIF-8, while the bands between 900 and 1350 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are attributed to the in-plane bending of the imidazolate ring (Basu & Balakrishnan, 2017). The bands that appeared below $800{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ belong to the out-of-plane bending of the imidazolate ring. Also, the bands in the region of 1310 to $1500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the stretching vibration of the ring (Esmaeili et al., 2022).
Fig. 1c illustrates the morphology of ZIF-8 structures obtained by SEM with 10,000 times magnification. The microstructure of ZIF-8 in­ cludes rhombic dodecahedron building units that are regularly placed next to each other in larger grape-like clusters. The similar microstruc­ ture has been addressed for ZIF-8 particles by other researchers (X.
Zhang et al., 2019). The average size of the ZIF-8 particles is approxi­ mately $350~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
## 3.2. Morphological characterization
After the production of bare and ZIF-8 particle-containing mem­ branes, different characterization techniques were applied to the membrane specimens. In order to characterize the surface and crosssectional morphology of the membranes, they were examined by SEM images. In Fig. 2 and Fig. S2 cross-sectional and surface scanning elec­ tron microscope (SEM) images of the fabricated membranes are given. As for cross-sectional view, it can be seen that all of the membranes have a finger-like structure (see Fig. 2). With the addition of particles, espe­ cially at higher magnifications, it was observed that the membrane sections display a denser structure. In addition, it can be seen that the finger-like pores are wider in particle added membranes. In this latter phenomenon, it can be ascribed to the increase in the mass transfer exchange rate between the solvent and the non-solvent with the addition of inorganic substances. Accordingly, wider channels are formed in the membrane (Karimi, Khataee, Vatanpour, & Safarpour, 2019a). Furthermore, the rate of change between solvent and non-solvent also affects the pore structure of the membrane. If the solvent and nonsolvent have high miscibility, the exchange rate between them will also be increased. This causes the formation of a finger-like structure. Since DMF used in this study is a water-miscible solvent, it can be ex­ pected that the obtained membranes will have finger-like structures (Cave et al., 2012; Mohsenpour et al., 2016).
After analyzing the membrane surface, it can be said that although there is no significant difference between the images, no adverse effects are observed on the membrane surface as a result of the addition of particles to the membrane. Since no agglomeration is detected on the surface, it can be thought that the particle is homogeneously dispersed in the membrane. Apart from that, the smooth surface of the membranes can be attributed to the low amount of ZIF-8 and its non-appearance on the surface due to its compatibility with the membrane (Karimi, Kha­ taee, et al., 2019a).
Table 1 lists the porosity, average pore diameter, contact angle, and viscosity of the polymer solution of the bare and ZIF-8 MOF doped membranes. It was seen that the bare membrane had the lowest porosity with $62.7~\%$ , in which by adding ZIF-8 MOF in $0.5~\%$ concentration increased membrane porosity by almost $0.9\%$ and reached its highest value at $71.7\%$ . In parallel with the increase in porosity, the mean pore size also increased reaching its maximum at $5.1\mathrm{nm}$ for $0.5\%$ ZIF-8 MOF concentration. The fact that ZIF-8 MOF containing membranes have higher porosity and average pore size supports the fact that the inor­ ganic addition in the polymer solution is effective in the coagulation process and improves the solvent and non-solvent exchange rate when added at proper concentrations (Ayyaru et al., 2020). In other words, ZIF-8 MOF could act as a catalyst in the coagulation process and support the formation of more pores (Karimi, Khataee, et al., 2019a). However, a slight decrease in porosity and mean pore diameter values were observed with a further increase in ZIF-8 MOF concentration. In addi­ tion, the molecular weight cut-off (MWCO) of the membranes was determined by the solute rejection of different molecular weights of polyethylene glycol (PEG) and inserted in Table 1. All membranes have the MWCO lower than $1100~\mathrm{Da}$ , confirming the ability of these mem­ branes for rejection of dyes and organic molecules.
To further explain this phenomenon, the viscosity values of the polymer solution were also considered. For instance, the viscosity increased continuously depending on the increase of ZIF-8 MOF con­ centration in the casting solution. However, the rise in viscosity hinders the solvent-nonsolvent exchange after a certain point. This challenge in the coagulation process leads to producing a denser membrane (Karimi, Khataee, Vatanpour, et al., 2020b). It can be concluded that if the $0.5\%$ ZIF-8 MOF concentration increased further, the solution viscosity was above $2.6\mathrm{Pa}.s.$ , decreasing the mean pore diameter and porosity.
Another parameter that has a significant effect on membrane per­ formance is surface hydrophilicity. According to the contact angle re­ sults reported in Table 1, the contact angle of the pristine membrane was $78^{\circ}$ . Interestingly, with the increase in ZIF-8 MOF concentration, the surface hydrophilicity increased, in tune with a decrease in the contact angle. In the $2\%$ ZIF-8 MOF doped membrane, the contact angle reached the lowest value at $60.5^{\circ}$ . In general, the contact angles of the doped membranes were lower than the bare membrane and measured in the range of $75.6^{\circ}-60.5^{\circ}$ . Even if ZIF-8 has been reported to be hydrophobic in its pristine form (it means with no chemical functionalization), it has demonstrated to improve the hydrophilicity once incorporated in polymer membranes due to changes in surface roughness due to its structure, as proven in previous studies (Wang et al., 2022; Wu et al., 2021). Moreover, hydrophilic groups on the ZIF-8 MOF surface, such as ${\tt N}\mathrm{-H}$ , promote the formation of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ layer on the surface of the doped membranes (supports superior H bonding), leading to a decrease in the contact angle (Karimi, Khataee, Vatanpour, et al., 2020b).
The surface roughness is one of the most crucial parameters affecting membrane fouling. Thus, the surface roughness morphology of the bare and ZIF-8 MOF doped membranes was determined by atomic force microscopy (AFM) imaging over a scan area of $8\times8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. 3). The average roughness $(S_{a})$ , root mean square roughness $(S_{q})$ , and the height difference between the highest peak and the lowest valley $(S_{z})$ are the surface roughness parameters, as listed in Table 2. Although the surface roughness of the bare membrane was the lowest, the surface roughness of the doped membranes increased consistently with increasing ZIF-8 MOF concentration. While the average roughness of the bare mem­ brane was $8.28~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , it increased to $52.75\ \mathrm{nm}$ at $2\%$ ZIF-8 MOF con­ centration, ranked the maximum level in this study. The reason for this may be that the viscosity of the polymer solution increases due to the increasing ZIF-8 MOF concentration. Therefore, the surface is rougher due to decreasing the rate of the solvent-nonsolvent exchange. The in­ cremental surface roughness increases the active membrane surface area and enhances the membrane flux. At the same time, it can increase the fouling rate as it also represents the surface area where adsorption oc­ curs. In addition, valleys allow pollutants to accumulate quickly. How­ ever, fouling may not increase at the same rate with increasing surface roughness because the flux adjuvant nature of ZIF-8 MOF nanocrystals represses the effect of fouling caused by increasing active surface area (Karimi, Khataee, et al., 2019a; Yan et al., 2006).
## 3.3. Membrane performance
As for membrane separation performance, pure water and BSA flux results of the composite membranes are shown in Fig. 4, along with their comparison with those of the bare membrane. The bare membrane offered a pure water and BSA flux of about 17.3 and $8.9\mathrm{~L~m~}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. Interestingly, $0.5~\%$ ZIF-8 MOF concentration resulted in the highest pure water and BSA flux values. In this case, the pure water flux increased by $104\%$ and reached $36.7\mathrm{L}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , while the BSA flux increased by $98~\%$ and reached $17.7\mathrm{L}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . When the ZIF-8 con­ centration was increased further, a slight decrease was observed in pure water flux, and BSA flux met a plateau. Nevertheless, in any case, higher pure water and BSA fluxes were obtained in the ZIF-8 MOF doped membranes compared to the bare membrane. The decrease in the fluxes due to the increase in ZIF-8 concentration was associated with particle agglomeration during membrane fabrication, as observed in previous studies (Rabiee et al., 2015; Wang et al., 2022). Another reason for flux declining is membrane porosity and mean pore size decrease due to the higher viscosity of the casting solution.
## 3.4. Antifouling performance evaluation
FRR values were calculated to investigate the reusability of the membranes; herein, an increase in FRR indicates improved antifouling properties (Dasgupta et al., 2014). Fig. 5 represents the antifouling properties of the bare and ZIF-8 MOF doped membranes. Obviously, all ZIF-8 MOF doped membranes exhibited better antifouling properties with higher FRR, resulting in a $9.1~\%$ increment compared to the bare membrane. Although the FRR percent increased when the ZIF-8 MOF addition increased up to $0.25\%$ , the lowest FRR value among the doped membranes was observed at $84.4\%$ when the ZIF-8 MOF concentration reached $0.5~\%$ . The low FRR of the $0.5~\%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane may be because it has the highest BSA flux. Since the treated water flow rate per unit time for the same membrane area is higher when compared to other doped membranes, the amount of pollutants retained on the surface is also higher. For this reason, over time, the pollutants move towards the pores and reach the areas that cannot be removed by physical cleaning. It is possible that this phenomenon caused the FRR value to be observed low. With the increase of ZIF-8 MOF concentration to $1\%$ and $2\%$ , the percentage of FRR increased relative to $0.5~\%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane. However, when compared with other MOF-containing membranes, it was seen that the FRRs were quite close to each other. In general, the FRR of the bare membrane was $83.4~\%$ , while that of the doped mem­ branes ranged from $84.8~\%$ to $91\%$ . A maximum of $93\ \%$ FRR was reached in the previous study, which produced a CA UF membrane with hydrous manganese oxide doped (Vetrivel et al., 2019). In another study, a maximum FRR of $89\%$ was obtained in emulsion poly­ vinylidene chloride/ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ doped UF membranes (Rabiee et al., 2014). Compared with a study examining the effect of $\mathtt{M O F@G O}$ doped CA membranes, a maximum FRR value of $88.13\%$ was obtained, which was lower than the one reported in this study(S. Yang et al., 2019).
In addition to pure water flux and FRR, membrane fouling ratio is another critical parameter determining membrane performance. For this reason, the $R_{t},R_{r},$ and $R_{i r}$ percent obtained from pure water and BSA filtration of pure and ZIF-8 doped membranes are comparatively shown in Fig. 6a.
In theory, poor interactions between contaminants and the mem­ brane surface can be overcome by physical cleaning in the case of reversible fouling. On the other hand, if these interactions are strong and cannot be overcome by simple physical cleaning, it is defined as irre­ versible fouling. Therefore, it is thought that the mechanism affecting the total fouling is mainly irreversible fouling (S. Yang et al., 2019). Among all blend membranes, the highest $R_{t},R_{r},$ and $R_{i r}$ were observed in the $0.5\%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane. On the contrary, $R_{i r}$ was lower in all of the ZIF-8 MOF doped membranes than the bare one, which means that the ZIF-8 MOF additive increases the membranes' reusability and pro­ longs their life. Another result obtained is that the $R r$ percentages are lower than those of the bare membrane, except for the $0.5\%$ ZIF-8/CA. The ZIF-8 MOF makes the membrane surface more hydrophilic and provides a compact water barrier, thus reducing the interactions be­ tween the contaminant and the membrane surface and reducing fouling. When the fouling rates are evaluated together with the FRR, the $0.5~\%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane having the lowest FRR among the doped mem­ branes confirms that it has the highest fouling rates. Therefore, in optimizing doped membranes, it is essential to consider the fouling rates and the flux to benefit from the membranes in the long term. The $0.5\%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane with the highest flux retains more contaminants per time, and the pollutants accumulated through the pores over time and decreased the FRR, which is consistent with ranking the highest $\mathtt{R}_{\mathrm{ir}}$ among the doped membranes.
Fig. 6b graphically shows the bare and doped membranes intrinsic, pore, cake, and total resistances. Clearly, the total resistance of the bare membrane is higher than that of the doped membranes. While the total resistance decreased with increasing ZIF-8 MOF concentration, it remained almost stable in membranes with ${>}0.5~\%$ ZIF-8. When the resistances of the doped membranes were evaluated among themselves, the lowest membrane resistance was observed at $0.5\ \%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane, confirming that the membrane had the highest flux. Cake resistance, on the other hand, did not show a noticeable decrease in 0.1 $\%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane but showed a significant decrease at higher concentrations. The cake resistance of the membranes was decreased by an increase of ZIF-8 concentration, apart from $0.5~\%$ ZIF-8 concentra­ tion. A slight increase in the ZIF-8/CA membrane may be caused by the fast accumulation of particles due to the highest BSA flux. As a result, it can be said that with the ZIF-8 MOF additive, an improvement was achieved in decreasing membrane resistance. In addition, the pore re­ sistances of the blend membranes were lower than the bare membrane in all cases. The pore resistances of the blend membranes were calculated as $9.5\times10^{12}$ , ${\bar{6.6}}\times10^{12}$ , $7.9\times10^{12}$ , $6.4\times10^{12}$ , and $5.9\times10^{12}$ , in the order of ZIF-8 concentration increase. The highest pore resistance be­ longs to the $0.5~\%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane, coinciding with having the highest irreversible fouling.
## 3.5. Dye/BSA separation
Dye and BSA removal experiments were carried out with the pro­ duced ZIF-8 doped membranes and bare CA membranes. Two different dyes, RB5 and RR120, were used in dye removal experiments at $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ ${\bf L}^{-1}$ concentration. BSA solution at a concentration of $500\ \mathrm{mg\L^{-1}}$ was also used. BSA and dye removal efficiencies for all membranes are given in Fig. 7. For instance, it can be seen that the BSA removal efficiency values are above $95\%$ for all the membranes. Although the BSA removal efficiencies of the bare CA membrane and the ZIF-8 membrane with a percentage of $0.5~\%$ are similar, it can be seen that the BSA removal efficiency values of the other ZIF-8 membranes are higher than those of the pure CA membrane. When the dye removal performances are examined, it can be understood that ZIF-8 added membranes provide a better removal efficiency than bare membranes. Especially in Reactive Red removal, it can be noted that the bare membrane achieved a dye removal of approximately $90~\%$ , while the removal efficiency of ZIF-8 added membranes is as high as $95\%$ and above.
In a study documented by Sun et al., ZIF-8 was modified with tannic acid to make ZIF-8 more hydrophilic, and a polysulfone (PSf) ultrafil­ tration membrane was produced with the obtained modified ZIF-8. In experiments with membranes produced by adding different ZIF-8 ratios, it was observed that BSA removal efficiencies ranged between 97 and 99 $\%$ on average (Sun et al., 2018).
In the study of Yang et al., ZIF-8/polyethyleneimine (PEI) hybrid NF membranes were fabricated. Experimentally, removal efficiency exper­ iments were carried out with 4 different dyes. Removal efficiencies of methyl blue, Congo red, acid fusion and methyl orange dyes were found to be approximately $99\%$ , $99\%$ , $94\%$ and $81\%$ , respectively (L. Yang, Wang, Zhang, 2017b). Since the molecular weights of methyl blue and congo red dyes are approximately 799 and $696\ g\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , respectively, and are closer to the dyes used in our study, despite the fact that the membrane in Yang et al.'s study is at the NF level, similar results can be observed.
## 3.6. Zinc release from the membranes including ZIF-8 particle
For measuring the leach of ZIF-8 from the membrane body, the zinc amount in the samples taken from the water bath, which the $1\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF8/CA was kept in it $40\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ immersed in $100\mathrm{mL}$ ), was analyzed using a Perkin Elmer Optima 3000 DV (ICP-OES) (USA). The examined storage times were 1, 3, 5, 7 and 10 days. According to Fig. S3, the Zn con­ centration is very low and after 5 days is almost constant. The low and constant amount of release established the stability of the ZIF-8 particles in the matrix of the CA membranes.
## 3.7. Comparison with literature data
Since it is needed to visualize the status of separation performance of these ZIF-8/CA membranes compared with the state-of the-art, this section is aimed to compare the results obtained within the scope of this article with those of the similar studies in the literature. In Table 3, the membrane performances of ZIF-8, which was also used in our study, and various other MOFs, as well as the polymer types and membrane types used together, are shown. As can be seen from the table, the NF mem­ branes produced in the current study succeeded in dye removal per­ formance. In addition, the FRR values obtained in the current work are higher than those obtained in most of the reported studies. Considering that ZIF-8 is mostly used in the modification of NF membranes in the literature, it can be said that the NF membranes produced in this study have comparable performance.
## 4. Conclusion
ZIF-8 MOF crystal structure was successfully synthesized and applied in CA nanofiltration membrane fabrication by phase separation tech­ nique. ZIF-8 MOF offered to form a compact water layer on the mem­ brane surface, which displayed a reduced interaction of contaminants with the membrane surface. Consequently, membranes with improved antifouling properties were obtained. As for separation performance, flux studies showed that pure water and BSA fluxes were enhanced by blending ZIF-8 MOF into the membranes. The highest pure water and BSA fluxes were 36.7 LMH and 17.7 LMH observed in $0.5~\%$ ZIF-8/CA membrane. Moreover, porosity, mean pore size, and the contact angle results supported the flux increment. However, a decrease in both fluxes, porosity, and mean pore diameter was observed when the ZIF-8 MOF concentration increased by ${>}0.5~\%$ . Depending on the increasing ZIF-8 MOF concentration, the viscosity of the polymer solution also increased; thus, the solvent non-solvent exchange rate decreased in the coagulation bath where phase inversion took place. The findings revealed that, BSA removal efficiency values were obtained above $95\%$ for all membranes. Moreover, it can be seen that the BSA removal effi­ ciency values of ZIF-8 membranes are higher than the bare CA mem­ brane. In terms of dye removal, it can be understood that ZIF-8 added membranes provide a better removal efficiency than bare membranes. Especially in RR120 removal, bare membrane achieved a dye removal of approximately $90\ \%$ , while the removal efficiency of ZIF-8 added membranes was found to be as high as $95\ \%$ . Another advantage of blending ZIF-8 in CA membrane is enhancing the antifouling properties. While the FRR of bare membrane had the lowest one, it was increased up to $91\%$ by blending ZIF-8 MOF. By addition of $0.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF-8 to the CA matrix, $9.0~\%$ porosity enhancement, $12.1^{\circ}$ reduction in water contact angle, $112\%$ increase in permeability, $5\%$ improvement of reversible fouling and $2.5\%$ increase in dye rejection was observed. In conclusion, the results of the study are evaluated in general, it can be seen that the use of ZIF-8 offers advantages in terms of removal efficiency and fouling mitigation to the membranes. Conducting treatment studies with real water will more clearly demonstrate the effect of the ZIF-8 additive on flux and fouling. We hope this study will pave the way for the real-scale usability of the ZIF-8/CA membranes.
